00:26:46,180 --> 00:26:48,690
 Good statement.

566
00:26:48,690 --> 00:26:51,610
 So the statement was that you can

567
00:26:51,610 --> 00:26:53,890
 have an agent on top of this.

568
00:26:53,890 --> 00:26:56,390
 So let's say you have 1,000 components.

569
00:26:56,390 --> 00:26:58,460
 This is like a marketplace for your agent.

570
00:26:58,460 --> 00:27:01,170
 You have 10,000 components.

571
00:27:01,170 --> 00:27:04,770
 And you tell your cloud code or your agent,

572
00:27:04,770 --> 00:27:10,450
 create a personal assistant for booking events here and there.

573
00:27:10,450 --> 00:27:12,810
 Then it will decide which components are useful

574
00:27:12,810 --> 00:27:15,160
 and create workflows on top of it.

575
00:27:15,160 --> 00:27:15,810
 Yeah?

576
00:27:15,810 --> 00:27:17,810
 Yeah.

577
00:27:17,810 --> 00:27:19,440
 By itself, by itself.

578
00:27:19,440 --> 00:27:22,780
 So I don't tell it a protocol of like, in the workflow,

579
00:27:22,780 --> 00:27:27,080
 I have step A to B happening first, and then step B,

580
00:27:27,080 --> 00:27:28,160
 and that's how you do it.

581
00:27:28,160 --> 00:27:30,280
 Or should I just tell it, create your own workflows?

582
00:27:30,280 --> 00:27:31,560
 Exactly, create your workflows.

583
00:27:31,560 --> 00:27:32,320
 But you can vet it.

584
00:27:32,320 --> 00:27:33,720
 So you create the workflows, and you can say,

585
00:27:33,720 --> 00:27:38,090
 oh, my god, this is a good nice workflow, or this is bad.

586
00:27:38,090 --> 00:27:39,710
 So here's the thing.

587
00:27:39,710 --> 00:27:43,080
 If you use upper cloud or cloud code,

588
00:27:43,080 --> 00:27:45,720
 you can even skip the part of components

589
00:27:45,720 --> 00:27:48,200
 and just say, cloud code, create workflows for me

590
00:27:48,200 --> 00:27:50,970
 to book AI events without giving it any components.

591
00:27:50,970 --> 00:27:53,430
 So you can go even higher level.

592
00:27:53,430 --> 00:27:55,350
 But I recommend against that, because then you

593
00:27:55,350 --> 00:27:57,040
 will have a lot of risks.

594
00:27:57,040 --> 00:28:01,420
 Because then it will create its own components in its own way.

595
00:28:01,420 --> 00:28:02,820
 You don't know if they're secure.

596
00:28:02,820 --> 00:28:04,660
 You don't know if you're going to do the right thing,

597
00:28:04,660 --> 00:28:05,570
 and so on.

598
00:28:05,570 --> 00:28:09,380
 So how many things in components first so you can vet them?

599
00:28:09,380 --> 00:28:12,250
 Because it's always nice to have each component to be audited.

600
00:28:12,250 --> 00:28:15,930
 So each component, you make sure it doesn't access data

601
00:28:15,930 --> 00:28:19,850
 that is sensitive.

602
00:28:19,850 --> 00:28:22,470
 Or you make sure it doesn't leak to your files or something.

603
00:28:22,470 --> 00:28:24,650
 So if you enforce your agent to build

604
00:28:24,650 --> 00:28:26,090
 workflows based on your components,

605
00:28:26,090 --> 00:28:30,820
 you already have some guarantees in terms of security and safety.

606
00:28:30,820 --> 00:28:34,650
 This is the trend of enterprise and organizations.

607
00:28:34,650 --> 00:28:38,280
 They're going from just asking cloud code to do things for you

608
00:28:38,280 --> 00:28:40,120
 to first build things in components,

609
00:28:40,120 --> 00:28:43,810
 and then cloud code can only do things based on those.

610
00:28:43,810 --> 00:28:44,730
 Very, very important.

611
00:28:44,730 --> 00:28:47,330
 You have more control to tell it which components you use.

612
00:28:47,330 --> 00:28:47,850
 Exactly.

613
00:28:47,850 --> 00:28:51,200
 You can go one step further.

614
00:28:51,200 --> 00:28:52,360
 You have these components.

615
00:28:52,360 --> 00:28:54,440
 You ask the agent to build a workflow for you.

616
00:28:54,440 --> 00:28:56,640
 Now you have a set of workflows.

617
00:28:56,640 --> 00:28:58,400
 You can also vet those.

618
00:28:58,400 --> 00:29:01,910
 So if you have a workflow for booking events,

619
00:29:01,910 --> 00:29:04,500
 you can just rerun that workflow.

620
00:29:04,500 --> 00:29:06,540
 If you don't have that workflow saved,

621
00:29:06,540 --> 00:29:07,500
 it will just create--

622
00:29:07,500 --> 00:29:09,790
 every time you ask cloud code to build a workflow,

623
00:29:09,790 --> 00:29:11,750
 it will create new workflows every time.

624
00:29:11,750 --> 00:29:13,580
 You're wasting tokens.

625
00:29:13,580 --> 00:29:17,740
 There's a lot of risks of doing something wrong.

626
00:29:17,740 --> 00:29:20,180
 Things are not predictable, because every time

627
00:29:20,180 --> 00:29:23,640
 it creates a workflow, even for the same domain,

628
00:29:23,640 --> 00:29:27,920
 for the same task, because all these models are probabilistic.

629
00:29:27,920 --> 00:29:28,920
 It's not deterministic.

630
00:29:28,920 --> 00:29:32,550
 You just recreate the workflow from scratch.

631
00:29:32,550 --> 00:29:35,750
 This is the idea of [INAUDIBLE] creating workflows from scratch.

632
00:29:35,750 --> 00:29:39,600
 So the more I'm thinking about it-- so basically,

633
00:29:39,600 --> 00:29:41,600
 the realistic use case, you're trying

634
00:29:41,600 --> 00:29:45,160
 to make a personal assistant for every department of your life.

